    "questionText": "A 62-year-old African-American man presents with a 2-month history of a dark brown band on his right thumbnail extending from the proximal nail fold to the distal free edge, with extension of pigmentation onto the proximal nail fold itself. The lecturer specifically distinguished this clinical finding from a benign mimic. Which of the following histologic findings explains this presentation?",
    "options": [
      { "text": "Increased melanin expression by normal-number melanocytes (pseudo-Hutchinson sign), a benign finding.", "explanation": "Incorrect. The classic Hutchinson's sign (pigment extension onto the proximal nail fold) signals increased melanocyte number — i.e., melanocytic proliferation — characteristic of acral lentiginous melanoma. Increased melanin expression alone (pseudo-Hutchinson's sign) is benign and lacks the periungual pigment extension. This would be the correct answer if asking the histologic basis of the benign mimic without periungual pigment." },
      { "text": "Loss of melanocytes due to autoimmune destruction, producing depigmentation.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Autoimmune destruction (vitiligo) causes depigmentation, not hyperpigmentation. This would be the correct answer if asking about patchy depigmented patches on the skin, not a dark nail band." },
      { "text": "Proliferation of melanocytes (Hutchinson's sign) — acral lentiginous melanoma, a serious skin cancer.", "explanation": "Correct. The lecturer specifically distinguished true Hutchinson's sign (pigment extension onto the proximal or lateral nail fold) as a sign of acral lentiginous melanoma — a serious cancer characterized by proliferation of melanocytes, not just increased melanin expression. The pigment extension beyond the nail itself onto the surrounding skin (periungual pigmentation) is the key clinical clue distinguishing true melanoma (Hutchinson's sign) from the benign pseudo-Hutchinson's sign. Acral lentiginous melanoma is the most common melanoma subtype in patients with darker skin and is often diagnosed late." },
      { "text": "Fungal infection of the nail plate (onychomycosis) producing pigment-mimicking debris.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Onychomycosis can produce dystrophic, thickened, yellow-brown nails but does not classically produce a linear longitudinal pigmented band with periungual extension. This would be the correct answer if the patient had crumbling, thickened nails with subungual hyperkeratosis." },
      { "text": "Subungual hematoma from acute trauma to the nail bed.", "explanation": "Incorrect. Subungual hematoma typically follows a discrete traumatic event and resolves as the nail grows out; it would not present as a stable longitudinal band with periungual extension over months. This would be the correct answer if the patient had a recent crush injury with sudden onset of subungual purple-black discoloration." }
    ],
